#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Prepare a PostgreSQL SQL dump for DuckDB at build time.

A thin fork of ../../../sqlite/scripts/pgsql/transform. Both hooks solve the
same problem -- several of our datasets ship only as PostgreSQL dumps -- but
DuckDB's parser *is* PostgreSQL-derived, so almost every rewrite the SQLite
hook performs is unnecessary here and would only lose fidelity. What is left is
the short list of things DuckDB genuinely cannot do, each verified against
DuckDB 1.5.5 (the pinned engine) with the actual dumps behind the eight tags
that use this hook (world, iso3166, frenchtowns, usda, pgexercises, dellstore,
northwind, sportsdb).

Kept, with the error DuckDB raises without it:

  * transcode to UTF-8. world / usda / dellstore ship Latin-1 bytes; DuckDB
    validates UTF-8 in string literals and aborts on the first accented town or
    author name.
  * ``COPY tbl (cols) FROM stdin`` + TEXT-format data + ``\\.`` -> batched
    multi-row ``INSERT``s. DuckDB has ``COPY``, but only from a *file*: there
    is no inline-data form, so the data rows parse as SQL and fail
    ("syntax error at or near ...").
  * drop ``SET ...``. Every spelling in these dumps fails: unknown parameters
    (``client_encoding``, ``default_with_oids``) raise a Catalog Error, and
    ``standard_conforming_strings = on`` / ``SET SESSION CHARACTERISTICS`` /
    the multi-value ``search_path = cd, pg_catalog`` are outright parse errors.
  * drop ``SELECT pg_catalog.setval(...)``: DuckDB has ``nextval``/``currval``
    but no ``setval`` (Catalog Error), and a fresh load never needs it -- the
    dumps' id columns are all written explicitly.
  * drop ``GRANT`` / ``REVOKE`` and psql's ``\\connect`` / ``\\.`` (parse errors),
    and ``CREATE``/``DROP SCHEMA`` together with ``public.`` / ``cd.``
    qualifiers, which flattens every dataset into DuckDB's default ``main``
    schema. (pgexercises is the one dump with a schema: it creates ``cd``,
    then relies on ``search_path`` -- which is dropped above -- for its
    unqualified ``CREATE TABLE``s while its indexes say ``cd.bookings``. Keeping
    the schema would leave tables in one place and indexes pointing at
    another.)
  * strip ``USING btree`` / ``USING hash`` / ``USING lsm`` from ``CREATE INDEX``
    (Binder Error: Unknown index type: BTREE), inline and on its own line --
    pgexercises' indexes wrap onto four lines.
  * strip ``WITH (OIDS ...)`` / ``WITHOUT OIDS`` (Not implemented Error).
  * strip ``::regclass``, the one PostgreSQL cast DuckDB has no type for
    (Catalog Error: Type with name regclass does not exist!). Stripping it
    rather than the whole ``DEFAULT nextval('s'::regclass)`` keeps the default
    working, since DuckDB does have sequences.
  * ``serial`` -> ``integer`` (and ``bigserial``/``smallserial``). DuckDB has no
    ``serial`` pseudo-type (Catalog Error: Type with name serial does not
    exist!); frenchtowns and dellstore declare their id columns with it and
    supply every value in the data, so a plain integer column is faithful.
  * drop table-level ``FOREIGN KEY`` constraints from ``CREATE TABLE``, with
    the comma that precedes them -- the same rewrite the scripts/chinook hook
    makes, for the same reason plus one more. DuckDB rejects referential
    actions outright ("FOREIGN KEY constraints cannot use CASCADE, SET NULL or
    SET DEFAULT", which is pgexercises' ``members.recommendedby``), and even
    with the actions stripped it validates the reference per *row* where
    PostgreSQL validates per statement -- so pgexercises' self-referencing
    multi-row ``INSERT`` (member 1 recommended by a member listed later) fails
    with a Constraint Error. Because the ``ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT ...
    FOREIGN KEY`` form is unsupported anyway (below), dropping these keeps FK
    handling uniform across both spellings.
  * ``ALTER TABLE`` is filtered rather than dropped wholesale (the SQLite hook
    can only drop): DuckDB implements ``ADD CONSTRAINT ... PRIMARY KEY``, so
    those survive and world/usda/dellstore keep their primary keys, while
    ``ADD CONSTRAINT ... FOREIGN KEY`` / ``UNIQUE`` (Not implemented Error: No
    support for that ALTER TABLE option yet!) and ``OWNER TO`` (parse error)
    are dropped.
  * drop PL/pgSQL stored functions -- dellstore's ``new_customer`` -- which
    DuckDB has no equivalent for.
  * drop ``CREATE DOMAIN`` blocks, to the terminating ``;`` (parse error).
    sportsdb declares ``primary_id AS integer`` and never uses it, so nothing
    downstream refers to the name.

Deleted from the SQLite hook, because DuckDB does not need them:

  * ``::type`` cast stripping. DuckDB speaks PostgreSQL casts; world's
    ``continent = 'Asia'::text`` CHECK constraint loads verbatim.
  * lower-casing table identifiers. DuckDB identifiers are case-insensitive
    (and case-preserving), so a dump that defines ``Regions`` and loads
    ``regions`` resolves either way -- frenchtowns does exactly that.
  * ``DEFAULT nextval(...)`` stripping and the ``CREATE``/``ALTER SEQUENCE``
    block drop: DuckDB has real sequences. (No dump here declares one
    standalone; ``serial`` is handled above.)
  * ``COMMENT ON`` dropping: DuckDB supports ``COMMENT ON``.
  * the ``ALTER TABLE ONLY`` -> ``ALTER TABLE`` rewrite: DuckDB accepts ``ONLY``.
  * the ``PRAGMA synchronous=OFF`` / ``journal_mode`` preamble, which is
    SQLite-only and would itself be a Catalog Error here.

A dataset whose dump needs more than the shared rules opts in via an
environment knob, set by a thin dataset-specific transform hook that then
delegates here (see duckdb/scripts/northwind, and sqlite/scripts/airlines for
the pattern). It defaults off, so the symlinked datasets' output is
byte-identical with or without it:

  PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA=1   rewrite the empty bytea literal ``'\\x'`` to ``''``
                        (DuckDB: "Invalid hex escape code"). Opt-in because
                        the hook has no column types, and in a *text* column
                        the same token is the literal two-character string
                        ``\\x`` -- see EMPTY_BYTEA_RE below.

Every other dataset symlinks its scripts/<dataset> directory to scripts/pgsql,
exactly like the SQLite side; the conversion is dataset-agnostic and keyed only
on $SQL_FILES (the files the loader stage cats into the duckdb CLI), which it
rewrites in place.
"""
import glob
import os
import re
import sys

BATCH = 500

# An empty PostgreSQL bytea is written `'\x'` -- the hex format's `\x` prefix
# with no digits after it. DuckDB's BLOB parser rejects it ("Conversion Error:
# Invalid hex escape code encountered in string -> blob conversion"), while a
# non-empty `'\x89AB'` converts fine; `''` is the same empty value spelled in a
# way both engines accept. northwind carries 17 of them (categories.picture,
# employees.photo).
#
# Opt-in via PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA=1, and applied to the file as text, because the
# hook has no column types and so cannot tell a bytea column from a text one:
# in a *text* column the identical source token is the literal two-character
# string `\x`, which an always-on rewrite would silently empty. The pattern is
# the entire literal -- quote, backslash, x, quote -- so `'\x89AB'`, a value
# that merely ends in `\x` (`'C:\x'`), and two adjacent literals that happen to
# abut (`'a\', 'x'`) are all untouched. The one false positive left is a text
# value that embeds `'\x'` between doubled quotes, which is exactly why this is
# per-dataset rather than unconditional.
EMPTY_BYTEA_RE = re.compile(r"'\\x'")

# A COPY header: COPY <table> (<cols>) FROM stdin [DELIMITER 'x'];
COPY_RE = re.compile(
    r"^COPY\s+([^\s(]+)\s*\(([^)]*)\)\s+FROM\s+stdin"
    r"(?:\s+DELIMITER\s+'([^']*)')?\s*;",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# Whole lines that DuckDB cannot parse or execute and that a fresh load does
# not need. See the module docstring for the error each one avoids.
DROP_LINE_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(SET\s|SELECT\s+pg_catalog\.setval|GRANT\s|REVOKE\s|"
    r"WITHOUT\s+OIDS|USING\s+(btree|hash|lsm)\b|\\connect|\\c\s|\\\."
    r"|CREATE\s+SCHEMA|DROP\s+SCHEMA|CREATE\s+DATABASE|DROP\s+DATABASE)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# CREATE DOMAIN has no DuckDB equivalent (parse error) and is dropped whole,
# to its terminating ';' -- the same buffered-block drop DROP_BLOCK_RE performs
# in the SQLite hook. sportsdb declares `CREATE DOMAIN primary_id AS integer;`
# and then never uses it (every column that would carry it is spelled
# `integer`/`serial` outright), so nothing downstream refers to the name.
DROP_BLOCK_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*CREATE\s+DOMAIN\b", re.IGNORECASE)

# ALTER TABLE is buffered to its terminating ';' and then filtered, because
# DuckDB implements exactly one of the forms these dumps use.
ALTER_TABLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*ALTER\s+TABLE\b", re.IGNORECASE)
ALTER_KEEP_RE = re.compile(r"\bADD\s+CONSTRAINT\b.*\bPRIMARY\s+KEY\b",
                           re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL)
ALTER_REJECT_RE = re.compile(r"\b(FOREIGN\s+KEY|UNIQUE)\b",
                             re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL)

# PL/pgSQL stored functions have no DuckDB translation.
FUNC_START_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*CREATE\s+(OR\s+REPLACE\s+)?FUNCTION\b",
                           re.IGNORECASE)
FUNC_END_RE = re.compile(r"\bLANGUAGE\s+\w+\s*;\s*$", re.IGNORECASE)

# `serial` and friends, as a standalone column type only -- never inside an
# identifier (`serial_no`) or a string.
SERIAL_RE = re.compile(r"\b(big|small)?serial(\d*)\b", re.IGNORECASE)
_SERIAL_TYPE = {"big": "bigint", "small": "smallint", "": "integer"}

# CREATE TABLE is buffered to its terminating ';' so table-level FOREIGN KEY
# constraints can be removed with their preceding comma (dropping the line
# alone would leave a dangling comma before the closing paren).
CREATE_TABLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*CREATE\s+TABLE\b", re.IGNORECASE)
# The referenced column list is optional: PostgreSQL lets `REFERENCES t` stand
# for "t's primary key", which is the spelling northwind's join tables use. The
# referenced table is matched as a single token rather than "anything up to the
# next '('" so that a column-list-less clause cannot swallow the whole of the
# *following* FOREIGN KEY line looking for one -- which left northwind's
# customer_customer_demo as `PRIMARY KEY (...) REFERENCES customers` and a
# parse error.
FK_RE = re.compile(
    r',\s*FOREIGN\s+KEY\s*\([^)]*\)\s*REFERENCES\s+[^\s(,]+(?:\s*\([^)]*\))?'
    r'(?:\s*ON\s+(?:DELETE|UPDATE)\s+'
    r'(?:NO\s+ACTION|CASCADE|RESTRICT|SET\s+NULL|SET\s+DEFAULT))*',
    re.IGNORECASE)


def configure():
    """(Re)read the environment knobs into module state.

    Called at import and again from main(), so both the exec'd hook and a test
    driving main() see the current environment (the SQLite hook's pattern).
    """
    global EMPTY_BYTEA
    EMPTY_BYTEA = os.environ.get("PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA", "") == "1"


configure()


def transcode(path):
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        raw = fh.read()
    try:
        return raw.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return raw.decode("latin-1")


def rewrite_empty_bytea(text):
    """`'\\x'` -> `''`, but only when PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA=1 (see the constant)."""
    return EMPTY_BYTEA_RE.sub("''", text) if EMPTY_BYTEA else text


def strip_schema(line):
    """Flatten `public.`/`cd.` qualifiers into DuckDB's default `main` schema."""
    return re.sub(r"(?i)\b(public|cd)\.", "", line)


def demote_serial(line):
    """`serial` -> `integer` (DuckDB has no serial pseudo-type)."""
    return SERIAL_RE.sub(
        lambda m: _SERIAL_TYPE[(m.group(1) or "").lower()], line)


def clean_ddl(line):
    """Apply dialect fixes to a single DDL/statement line."""
    line = strip_schema(line)
    line = re.sub(r"(?i)\busing\s+(btree|hash|lsm)\b", "", line)
    line = re.sub(r"(?i)\bwith\s*\(\s*oids[^)]*\)", "", line)
    line = re.sub(r"(?i)::regclass\b", "", line)
    line = demote_serial(line)
    return line


def drop_foreign_keys(statement):
    """Remove table-level FOREIGN KEY constraints from a CREATE TABLE block."""
    return FK_RE.sub("", statement)


def split_copy_row(line, delim):
    """Split a COPY TEXT row on unescaped delimiters, keeping escape pairs."""
    fields, cur, i, n = [], [], 0, len(line)
    while i < n:
        c = line[i]
        if c == "\\" and i + 1 < n:
            cur.append(line[i:i + 2]); i += 2; continue
        if c == delim:
            fields.append("".join(cur)); cur = []; i += 1; continue
        cur.append(c); i += 1
    fields.append("".join(cur))
    return fields


_UNESCAPE = {"t": "\t", "n": "\n", "r": "\r", "\\": "\\"}


def copy_value(field):
    """Render one COPY field as a SQL string literal."""
    if field == "\\N":
        return "NULL"
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(field):
        if field[i] == "\\" and i + 1 < len(field):
            out.append(_UNESCAPE.get(field[i + 1], field[i + 1])); i += 2
        else:
            out.append(field[i]); i += 1
    return "'" + "".join(out).replace("'", "''") + "'"


def keep_alter(statement):
    """True for the one ALTER TABLE form DuckDB implements: ADD PRIMARY KEY."""
    return bool(ALTER_KEEP_RE.search(statement)
                and not ALTER_REJECT_RE.search(statement))


def ends_statement(line, in_string):
    """Track quote state across a line; report whether a ';' closes outside it.

    Multi-row ``INSERT``s (pgexercises ships its data that way) span many lines
    and every one of them is *data*, so they must reach the output untouched --
    running them through clean_ddl would rewrite a value that happens to say
    "serial" or "cd.". Knowing where the statement ends needs quote state, not
    a bare ``';' in line``: a literal may itself contain a semicolon, and a
    doubled ``''`` inside one is an escaped quote, not a close.
    """
    i, n, done = 0, len(line), False
    while i < n:
        c = line[i]
        if in_string:
            if c == "'" and i + 1 < n and line[i + 1] == "'":
                i += 2; continue
            if c == "'":
                in_string = False
        elif c == "'":
            in_string = True
        elif c == ";":
            done = True
        i += 1
    return in_string, done


def read_statement(lines, i):
    """Collect lines[i:] up to the ';' that closes the statement.

    Returns the joined statement and the index just past it.
    """
    block = [lines[i]]
    in_string, done = ends_statement(lines[i], False)
    while not done and i + 1 < len(lines):
        i += 1
        block.append(lines[i])
        in_string, done = ends_statement(lines[i], in_string)
    return "\n".join(block), i + 1


def convert_file(text, out):
    lines = text.split("\n")
    i, n = 0, len(lines)
    drop_func = False
    while i < n:
        line = lines[i]

        if drop_func:
            if FUNC_END_RE.search(line):
                drop_func = False
            i += 1; continue

        m = COPY_RE.match(line)
        if m:
            table, cols, delim = m.group(1), m.group(2), m.group(3) or "\t"
            table = table.split(".")[-1]
            head = "INSERT INTO {} ({}) VALUES\n".format(table, cols)
            batch = []
            i += 1
            while i < n and lines[i].rstrip() != "\\.":
                row = split_copy_row(lines[i], delim)
                batch.append("(" + ", ".join(copy_value(f) for f in row) + ")")
                if len(batch) >= BATCH:
                    out.write(head + ",\n".join(batch) + ";\n")
                    batch = []
                i += 1
            if batch:
                out.write(head + ",\n".join(batch) + ";\n")
            i += 1  # skip the \. terminator
            continue

        if FUNC_START_RE.match(line):
            drop_func = not FUNC_END_RE.search(line)
            i += 1; continue

        if CREATE_TABLE_RE.match(line):
            # Buffered whole so a table-level FOREIGN KEY can be removed along
            # with the comma that precedes it -- a per-line drop would leave a
            # dangling comma before the closing paren.
            statement, i = read_statement(lines, i)
            out.write(drop_foreign_keys(clean_ddl(statement)) + "\n")
            continue

        if ALTER_TABLE_RE.match(line):
            # Buffered too (pg_dump wraps ALTER TABLE over two lines), because
            # the verb that decides whether DuckDB can run it -- PRIMARY KEY vs
            # FOREIGN KEY / UNIQUE / OWNER TO -- is on the continuation line.
            statement, i = read_statement(lines, i)
            if keep_alter(statement):
                out.write(clean_ddl(statement) + "\n")
            continue

        if DROP_BLOCK_RE.match(line):
            # Buffered and dropped whole: a CREATE DOMAIN may wrap onto a
            # second line (a CHECK or a DEFAULT), and half of one left behind
            # is a parse error of its own.
            _, i = read_statement(lines, i)
            continue

        if DROP_LINE_RE.match(line):
            i += 1; continue

        stripped = line.lstrip()
        if stripped.startswith("--") or not stripped:
            out.write(line + "\n")
        elif stripped.upper().startswith("INSERT INTO"):
            # Only the header names the table (and so may carry a schema
            # qualifier); every following line is data, copied verbatim.
            out.write(strip_schema(line) + "\n")
            in_string, done = ends_statement(line, False)
            while not done and i + 1 < n:
                i += 1
                out.write(lines[i] + "\n")
                in_string, done = ends_statement(lines[i], in_string)
            i += 1; continue
        else:
            out.write(clean_ddl(line) + "\n")
        i += 1


def main():
    configure()
    files = os.environ.get("SQL_FILES", "").split() or sorted(glob.glob("*.sql"))
    files = [f for f in files if os.path.isfile(f)]
    if not files:
        sys.exit("pgsql transform: no SQL files to convert")

    for path in files:
        text = rewrite_empty_bytea(transcode(path))
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as out:
            convert_file(text, out)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
